Lab 12: Deploying Camel Routes on OpenShift
Objectives
Containerize Apache Camel routes and deploy them on OpenShift
Integrate CI/CD pipelines for automated deployment
Configure OpenShift's native service discovery and load balancing for Camel routes
Prerequisites
OpenShift CLI (oc) installed and configured
Podman or Docker installed
Basic understanding of Apache Camel and Kubernetes concepts
Access to an OpenShift cluster with developer permissions
Maven installed (for Java-based Camel routes)
Git installed for version control
Lab Setup
Verify your OpenShift access:

oc login <openshift_cluster_url> -u <username> -p <password>
oc project <your_project_name>
Clone the sample Camel project:

git clone https://github.com/apache/camel-examples.git
cd camel-examples/examples/camel-example-spring-boot
Task 1: Containerize and Deploy Camel Routes
Subtask 1.1: Build Camel Application
Package the Spring Boot Camel application:

mvn clean package
Verify the JAR file was created:

ls target/*.jar
Subtask 1.2: Create Container Image
Create a Dockerfile:

FROM eclipse-temurin:17-jdk
COPY target/camel-example-spring-boot-*.jar /app.jar
ENTRYPOINT ["java","-jar","/app.jar"]
Build the image with Podman:

podman build -t camel-spring-boot .
Tag and push to a container registry:

podman tag camel-spring-boot quay.io/<your-username>/camel-spring-boot:latest
podman push quay.io/<your-username>/camel-spring-boot:latest
Subtask 1.3: Deploy to OpenShift
Create a new app from your image:

oc new-app quay.io/<your-username>/camel-spring-boot:latest
Expose the service:

oc expose svc/camel-spring-boot
Verify deployment:

oc get pods
oc logs <pod-name>
Expected Outcome: The Camel application should be running in a pod, and you should see route startup messages in the logs.

Task 2: CI/CD Pipeline Integration
Subtask 2.1: Set Up OpenShift Pipeline
Create a new file named pipeline.yaml:

apiVersion: tekton.dev/v1beta1
kind: Pipeline
metadata:
  name: camel-deployment-pipeline
spec:
  workspaces:
  - name: source
  tasks:
  - name: build
    taskRef:
      name: maven
    workspaces:
    - name: source
      workspace: source
  - name: deploy
    taskRef:
      name: openshift-client
    params:
    - name: SCRIPT
      value: |
        oc new-app --image-stream=camel-spring-boot --name=camel-app
        oc expose svc/camel-app
    runAfter:
    - build
Apply the pipeline:

oc apply -f pipeline.yaml
Subtask 2.2: Trigger Pipeline
Create a PipelineRun:

tkn pipeline start camel-deployment-pipeline --workspace name=source,emptyDir=""
Monitor pipeline progress:

tkn pipelinerun logs -f
Expected Outcome: The pipeline should build and deploy your Camel application automatically.

Task 3: Service Discovery and Load Balancing
Subtask 3.1: Configure Service Discovery
Verify the created service:

oc get svc camel-spring-boot
Access another service from your Camel route:

from("timer:foo?period=5000")
  .to("http://another-service:8080/api")
  .log("Response: ${body}");
Subtask 3.2: Configure Load Balancing
Scale your deployment:

oc scale deployment/camel-spring-boot --replicas=3
Verify load balancing:

watch oc get endpoints camel-spring-boot
Expected Outcome: Requests should be automatically load balanced across all available pods.

Troubleshooting Tips
If pods fail to start, check logs with oc logs <pod-name>
For image pull errors, verify your container registry credentials
If services can't communicate, check network policies with oc get networkpolicy
For pipeline failures, use tkn pipelinerun describe <run-name>
Conclusion
In this lab, you successfully:

Containerized a Camel application and deployed it to OpenShift
Implemented a CI/CD pipeline for automated deployments
Configured OpenShift's native service discovery and load balancing
The deployed Camel routes are now running in a scalable, managed environment with automated deployment capabilities.

